[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Spring Boot CLI: Rapid Application Development"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Spring Boot CLI (Command Line Interface) is a powerful tool for rapid application development with Spring. It allows you to quickly prototype and develop Spring Boot applications using Groovy scripts. This tutorial will guide you through the installation, usage, and key features of the Spring Boot CLI."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Installation"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before you can use the Spring Boot CLI, you need to install it. The installation process varies depending on your operating system."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Installing on macOS with Homebrew"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"If you have Homebrew installed, you can install the Spring Boot CLI with the following command:"},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"brew install springboot"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Installing on Linux with SDKMAN!"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"SDKMAN! (Software Development Kit Manager) is a tool for managing parallel versions of multiple Software Development Kits on most Unix based systems. You can install the Spring Boot CLI using SDKMAN!:"},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"sdk install springboot"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Installing Manually"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can also install the Spring Boot CLI manually by downloading the distribution from the Spring website and configuring your PATH environment variable. Here's a general outline:"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Download the latest Spring Boot CLI distribution from the Spring website (search for 'Spring Boot CLI download')."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Extract the downloaded archive to a directory of your choice."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Add the 'bin' directory of the extracted archive to your PATH environment variable.  For example, on Linux/macOS, you might add `export PATH=$PATH:/path/to/spring-boot-cli/bin` to your `.bashrc` or `.zshrc` file."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Verify the installation by running `spring --version` in your terminal."},{"order":17,"type":"heading","text":"Basic Usage"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once installed, you can use the `spring` command to create and run Spring Boot applications. The CLI primarily uses Groovy scripts for development, but Java is also supported."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating a Simple Application"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Let's create a simple web application that displays 'Hello, World!'. Create a file named `app.groovy` with the following content:"},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"@RestController
class App {
    @RequestMapping('/')
    String home() {
        "Hello, World!"
    }
}"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"Now, run the application using the following command:"},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"spring run app.groovy"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"The CLI will automatically download the necessary dependencies and start the application. Open your browser and navigate to `http://localhost:8080` to see the 'Hello, World!' message."},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Java Instead of Groovy"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"While Groovy is the preferred language for rapid prototyping with the Spring Boot CLI, you can also use Java. Create a file named `App.java` with the following content:"},{"order":27,"type":"code","text":"import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;

@SpringBootApplication
@RestController
public class App {

    @RequestMapping("/")
    public String home() {
        return "Hello, World!";
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(App.class, args);
    }
}"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"Run the application using:"},{"order":29,"type":"code","text":"spring run App.java"},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"The CLI will compile and run the Java code."},{"order":31,"type":"heading","text":"Dependency Management"},{"order":32,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Spring Boot CLI simplifies dependency management by automatically resolving dependencies based on the annotations and classes used in your code. However, you can also explicitly specify dependencies using the `@Grab` annotation in Groovy or by creating a `pom.xml` file for Java projects."},{"order":33,"type":"subheading","text":"Using @Grab in Groovy"},{"order":34,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `@Grab` annotation allows you to specify dependencies directly in your Groovy script. For example, to add the Spring Data JPA dependency, you can use the following:"},{"order":35,"type":"code","text":"@Grab('org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-data-jpa')

import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.EnableAutoConfiguration
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration

@Configuration
@EnableAutoConfiguration
class Application {
    static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(Application, args)
    }
}"},{"order":36,"type":"subheading","text":"Using pom.xml for Java"},{"order":37,"type":"paragraph","text":"For Java projects, you can create a `pom.xml` file to manage dependencies. The Spring Boot CLI will automatically recognize and use the `pom.xml` file. Here's a basic example:"},{"order":38,"type":"code","text":"<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <groupId>com.example</groupId>
    <artifactId>my-app</artifactId>
    <version>1.0-SNAPSHOT</version>

    <parent>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-parent</artifactId>
        <version>2.7.0</version>
        <relativePath/> <!-- lookup parent from repository -->
    </parent>

    <dependencies>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
                <artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>

</project>"},{"order":39,"type":"heading","text":"Advanced Features"},{"order":40,"type":"subheading","text":"Application Properties"},{"order":41,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can configure your application using `application.properties` or `application.yml` files. The Spring Boot CLI will automatically load these files when running your application."},{"order":42,"type":"paragraph","text":"For example, to change the port number, create an `application.properties` file with the following content:"},{"order":43,"type":"code","text":"server.port=9000"},{"order":44,"type":"subheading","text":"Customizing the Run Command"},{"order":45,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `spring run` command accepts various options to customize the execution of your application. For example, you can specify profiles, properties, and more."},{"order":46,"type":"code","text":"spring run --spring.profiles.active=dev app.groovy"},{"order":47,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":48,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Spring Boot CLI is a valuable tool for rapid application development. Its ease of use and automatic dependency management make it ideal for prototyping and developing Spring Boot applications quickly. By leveraging Groovy scripts and the `spring run` command, you can significantly reduce the development time and focus on building your application's logic."}]
